@meta
id: 11-11
title: Next match, then dot
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: dot-repeat, dot-design
par: 24
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl+F for dead, then Enter and Ctrl+Shift+K five times
open: flags.ts

@brief
Five feature flags in this file are marked `// dead`. The release train
cannot leave with them in. Delete those five lines and leave the other
ten alone.

They are scattered, and counting `j` presses between them would be slow
and easy to get wrong. What you want is to travel by what the lines say
rather than by where they are.

`/word` and `Enter` jumps to the next line containing that word, and `n`
jumps to the one after that. Chapter 12 does searching properly. Here it
is only a way to move, and the interesting part is what happens when you
alternate it with `.`.

@keys
| `/dead` `Enter` | jump to the next line containing `dead` |
| `n` | the next one after that |
| `dd` | delete the whole line |
| `.` | repeat the delete |

@hints
Do not count lines. Search for the marker instead, and delete the line you
land on.
--
`n` is to searching what `;` is to `f` — it repeats the jump without you
retyping the pattern. And like `;`, it leaves the dot register alone.
--
`/dead` `Enter`, then `dd`. After that the file is five keystrokes of
`n.`, `n.`, `n.`, `n.` away from done.

@solution
    /dead<Enter>  dd   n.  n.  n.  n.

`n.` is `;.` with the whole file in range instead of one line. Same idea,
wider net: the search finds the next place the edit is needed, the dot
performs it, and neither key knows or cares about the other.

Two things make this better than the find-and-replace you would have
reached for in VS Code. The first is that the edit is not limited to the
text you searched for. You searched for `dead` and you deleted an entire
line — you could just as well have indented it, commented it out or
changed the value three columns to its left. A replace can only replace.

The second is that you see each match before you commit to it. Press `n`,
look, and if this one is a false positive press `n` again instead of `.`.
Nothing happens until you say so. That inspection step is what makes
`n.` safe on a file you do not know well, and it is the whole subject of
lesson 11-14.

The cost is that `n.` cannot be automatic: you have to press `.` once per
match and count nothing. The next lesson removes even that.

@start flags.ts
export const flags = {
  refundWindow30Days: true,
  legacyRefundEndpoint: false, // dead
  receiptTemplateV4: true,
  batchReconcile: true,
  cardTokenCache: false, // dead
  sepaPayouts: false,
  webhookSignatureCheck: true,
  legacyCustomerSearch: false, // dead
  orderTimelineApi: true,
  emailDigest: false,
  ledgerShadowWrites: false, // dead
  refundWebhookRetry: true,
  csvExportV2: true,
  legacyStatusStrings: false, // dead
  paymentRetryBackoff: true,
};

@expected flags.ts
export const flags = {
  refundWindow30Days: true,
  receiptTemplateV4: true,
  batchReconcile: true,
  sepaPayouts: false,
  webhookSignatureCheck: true,
  orderTimelineApi: true,
  emailDigest: false,
  refundWebhookRetry: true,
  csvExportV2: true,
  paymentRetryBackoff: true,
};
